---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import burnout from "../assets/burnout.jpg";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
	<div class="p-4 flex items-center justify-center">
		<h1 class="text-4xl bg-ctp-mantle p-8 rounded-2xl">Burnout</h1>
	</div>
	<div class="flex flex-row flex-1 items-center justify-center flex-wrap gap-16 bg-ctp-mantle m-8 p-8 rounded-2xl">
		<div class="w-lg h-auto">
			<Image
				class="object-contain inline"
				src={burnout}
				alt="Photo by Nataliya Vaitkevich from Pexels: https://www.pexels.com/photo/overworked-employee-lying-in-front-of-laptop-6837643/"
			/>
		</div>
		<p class="text-xl">
			A big issue with our current long hours is burnout. Long, boring, difficult hours in a job, or any task, can wear a person down, lessening their quality
			of life. This can lead to workers feeling like their work has no value, doubting their abilities, and can even lead to physical issues like headaches and
			stomach problems (Job Burnout). These can make working harder, and impact a person’s entire life, not just their work. While long hours aren’t simply
			causing burnout themselves, they play a heavy factor when it comes to causing burnout, alongside a poor work-life balance, which can also be a result of
			long work hours (Job Burnout). Some of the other issues stemming from burnout include heart disease, depression, insomnia, and can make workers more
			susceptible to illness (Workplace Burnout). While some jobs do care about the mental health of their workers, there are often vast improvements that can
			be made to prevent burnout, such as checking in and keeping track of workloads and workers to ensure they aren’t being overworked and overwhelmed
			(Workplace Burnout). These causes and effects aren’t limited to any one type of job, although they may be more common in office jobs, due to the
			repetition of monotonous tasks. Some of the common ways to counter burnout involve self care, prioritizing social relationships, and simply taking breaks
			from anything related to work (Workplace Burnout). None of these things are difficult for the average person to do, but they all have the same issue of
			taking time and energy to do. That is time and energy that might be missing for a lot of people, as all of their time is spent either working or sleeping.
			Shortening the work day isn’t a perfect solution to all of these issues, but it would greatly benefit the lives of workers who might be dealing with
			burnout. The extra 2 hours in the day could be used to meet with friends, who would also have more free time outside of work themselves. If a person is
			short on sleep, they could devote the extra time to that. With people spending less time at work to begin with, they would be dealing with the stress of a
			workplace for a much shorter amount of time, which would be a relief from a portion of the mental effects of their job. With there being less burnout to
			counteract, and more time to counteract it, a slightly shorter workday would lead to a massive reduction in burnout for a huge number of workers.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div class="flex items-center justify-center">
		<a href="/" class="underline text-2xl bg-ctp-mantle p-4 rounded-2xl">back</a>
	</div>
	<div class="flex items-center justify-center">
		<a href="/crashes" class="underline text-2xl bg-ctp-mantle p-4 rounded-2xl">next</a>
	</div>
</Layout>
